---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Ethotechnics Institute — Open commons for accountable AI governance"
  description="The open-access Institute for the Ethotechnics framework: standards, glossary, ontology, API, diagnostics, and field notes for high-stakes AI systems."
  structuredDataType="collection"
>
  <section class="stack-lg">
    <p class="eyebrow">Ethotechnics Institute</p>
    <h1>The open commons for accountable AI governance</h1>
    <p class="section-lead">
      The Ethotechnics Institute is the public, open-access arm of the Ethotechnics framework. It
      publishes formal standards, a governance ontology, practitioner glossary, and field
      diagnostics for teams building or auditing high-stakes AI systems. Everything here is free
      to use, cite, and build on.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>
        For client engagements, facilitation, and advisory programs, see
        <a href="https://ethotechnics.com" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          Ethotechnics Studio →
        </a>.
      </em>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-lg" aria-labelledby="glossary-start-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
    <h2 id="glossary-start-heading">New to Ethotechnics? Start with the glossary.</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/glossary">Glossary →</a> — 40+ defined terms covering burden, bindingness,
      escalation authority, care infrastructure, and decision accountability. Each entry includes
      scope notes and citations across philosophy, STS, and governance literature.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-lg" aria-labelledby="ontology-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Core contribution</p>
    <h2 id="ontology-heading">Governance Ontology &amp; Reference API</h2>
    <p>
      A formal vocabulary of 40+ concepts used in accountability, burden, and care-infrastructure
      governance — with scope notes, citations from moral philosophy, STS, and organizational
      theory, and a read-only API for consuming canonical definitions in audits, tools, and
      research. The ontology is versioned and citable.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/glossary">Browse the ontology →</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>
      <a href="/api">View API reference →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-lg" aria-labelledby="network-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Community</p>
    <h2 id="network-heading">Ethotechnics Network</h2>
    <p>
      A small group of researchers, practitioners, and engineers working on burden measurement,
      care infrastructure, and AI accountability. Members receive advance access to new patterns
      and diagnostics, invitations to working sessions, and opportunities to co-author standards.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/participate">Apply to join →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-lg" aria-labelledby="citation-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Citation guidance</p>
    <h2 id="citation-heading">How to cite Ethotechnics</h2>
    <p>Ethotechnics Institute materials are published under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. To cite the framework:</p>
    <blockquote>
      Jain, K. (2025). <em>Ethotechnics: A governance and care-infrastructure framework for
      high-stakes systems.</em> Ethotechnics Institute. https://ethotechnics.org
    </blockquote>
    <p>Individual glossary entries and patterns include their own citation metadata.</p>
    <p>
      <a href="/about">About the framework →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-lg" aria-labelledby="recently-published-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">What’s new</p>
    <h2 id="recently-published-heading">Recently published</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/diagnostics/evidence-pack-readiness">Evidence Pack Readiness diagnostic</a> —
        v1.2 (March 2026)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/glossary">Glossary update</a>: 8 new entries on decision latency and repair
        authority (February 2026)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/field-notes">Field note</a>: Mapping invisible maintenance work in clinical AI
        (January 2026)
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
